import RequestProject.SL25Table

/-!
# Remark 2: the group of the presentation `A` is `SL(2,5)`

Remark 2 of the paper uses the presentation

  `A = ⟨x, y | x²yx⁻¹y, xy⁴xy⁻¹⟩`,  `G(A) ≅ SL(2,5)`,

whose exponent-sum matrix has determinant `-1`, so that `K(A)` is acyclic
(`RequestProject/ExampleSL25.lean`), while `G(A)` has torsion, so `K(A)` is not aspherical.

This file proves the quoted isomorphism `G(A) ≅ SL(2,5)`, using the 240 coset-table
identities of `RequestProject/SL25Table.lean`.  The two halves are:

* an upper bound `|G| ≤ 120` for *every* group generated by two elements satisfying the
  relators (`surjective_Wv`, `card_le_120`): the 120 coset words cover the group, because
  the table shows the set of their values is stable under right multiplication by the
  generators and their inverses;
* the homomorphism `G(A) → SL(2,5)` sending the generators to two explicit matrices, which
  is injective and surjective because the 120 words already take 120 distinct matrix
  values.

The final statement is `presentedA_mulEquiv_SL25`.  As a consequence `G(A)` has elements of
finite order, for instance `x` has order `6` (`orderOf_x`), which is the property of the
example that Remark 2 actually uses.
-/

set_option maxRecDepth 1000000
set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000

set_option backward.defeqAttrib.useBackward true
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

